{
	"version": 1,
	"cells": [
		{
			"kind": "markdown",
			"language": "markdown",
			"source": "# Three services, one notebook\n\nEvery cell here is a question the facts alone cannot answer, so the engine asks the service that declares the fact instead of asking you. The cells share one session, so run them in order with **Shift+Enter**.\n\nNothing reaches the network until the `/online` cell below is run. Skip it and the same questions ask you for a value instead, which is what a notebook does with no services at all. Once it has run, each question is a real request, and each fact a service answers comes back carrying `# <service>/<endpoint> <day>` in its line. Started with `--offline`, `/online` refuses, and the questions ask you either way."
		},
		{
			"kind": "markdown",
			"language": "markdown",
			"source": "## 0. Ask for the services\n\nA notebook is opened to read and to write, so a cell never sends a request nobody asked for: `/online` is the switch that attaches the registry the service maps declare - twelve facts a question can ask them for. One cell turns it on for the rest."
		},
		{
			"kind": "code",
			"language": "micro-expert-kb",
			"source": "/online"
		},
		{
			"kind": "markdown",
			"language": "markdown",
			"source": "## 1. ipwho.is - where this machine is\n\n`ipwho.is`'s lookup declares that it answers `has-location(session, ?place)`. Nothing is typed: the question is the whole request, and the answer is a place term with its continent, country, region, city and coordinates hung off it."
		},
		{
			"kind": "code",
			"language": "micro-expert-kb",
			"source": "has-location(session, ?place)"
		},
		{
			"kind": "markdown",
			"language": "markdown",
			"source": "One reply carried every fact about that place, so a second question about it reads what the session already holds rather than asking again. A line that is only a grouping is a rule body with no head: the two conditions are proved together, the first settling the place the second asks about, so the latitude is answered without the place's uid ever being typed."
		},
		{
			"kind": "code",
			"language": "micro-expert-kb",
			"source": "and(has-location(session, ?loc), has-latitude(?loc, ?lat))"
		},
		{
			"kind": "markdown",
			"language": "markdown",
			"source": "## 2. geocode.maps.co - a place by its name\n\nThis map answers about a place the session already names: the search is asked by the place's own `has-name` or `has-city`, and the reverse by its coordinates. A cell can type both halves, so the fact and the question that needs it can sit together. Two places are in the cell for a reason: `geocode.maps.co` answers Fort Worth, while Cairo's reply names the city `القاهرة`, so the search declines a name it cannot match - and with no key set it declines for that reason instead - and the other map that declares a place's coordinates, Open-Meteo's geocoder, answers. Either way the cell ends holding coordinates, each annotated with the map that gave it:"
		},
		{
			"kind": "code",
			"language": "micro-expert-kb",
			"source": "has-city(cairo, \"Cairo\")\nhas-latitude(cairo, ?lat)\n\nhas-city(fort-worth, \"Fort Worth\")\nhas-latitude(fort-worth, ?lat)"
		},
		{
			"kind": "markdown",
			"language": "markdown",
			"source": "The coordinates that answer came from are facts of the session now, so a question about the place is answered from them rather than asked again. The map's other half is its reverse endpoint, which reads an address back from a coordinate - the road and house number a city's own search result has no way to carry. The reverse is built from the place's coordinates, so this cell types them the way the search cell typed the city, and it has what it needs when it is run on its own. It is also the one cell here that wants the map's key, `GEOCODE_MAPS_API_KEY`: without it the map says which variable is missing and the cell asks you for the road instead of telling you one."
		},
		{
			"kind": "code",
			"language": "micro-expert-kb",
			"source": "has-latitude(fort-worth, 32.753177)\nhas-longitude(fort-worth, -97.332746)\nhas-road(fort-worth, ?road)"
		},
		{
			"kind": "markdown",
			"language": "markdown",
			"source": "## 3. Open-Meteo - the weather for a located place\n\nThe forecast is asked with the place's coordinates for the one variable a question names, for the next twenty-four hours of it, so a weather question is answered with a day of readings rather than with one:"
		},
		{
			"kind": "code",
			"language": "micro-expert-kb",
			"source": "## 3. Open-Meteo - the weather for a located place\n\nThe forecast is asked with the place's coordinates for the one variable a question names, for the next twenty-four hours of it, so the answer is a day of readings - one line an hour, each carrying the hour it covers:"
		},
		{
			"kind": "markdown",
			"language": "markdown",
			"source": "Every reading carries the hour it describes as its `@` and the day it was fetched in its annotation, and each variable's unit is a fact of the session beside them - `has-unit(temperature_2m, celsius)` - since a number alone does not say which unit it is in. A day is twenty-four such lines, and the forecast answers the other four documented variables the same way, one variable a question: `examples/report.kbnb` is where a day and the weeks after it are read as a report. Fetch the same fact again on another day and the newer reading replaces the line the earlier one stood on; a question that finds only an earlier day's reading asks the service again, and says which day it answered from when nothing can refresh it.\n\nTwo maps declare a place's coordinates - `geocode.maps.co`'s search and Open-Meteo's geocoder - so the one that cannot answer leaves the other to try before you are asked.\n\n`/facts` lists what the session holds now, provider facts and their annotations included:"
		},
		{
			"kind": "code",
			"language": "micro-expert-kb",
			"source": "/facts"
		}
	]
}
